  <body lang="EN-UK" style="tab-interval:36.0pt">
    <!--Institution: --><!--Moodle language: en--><!--Moodle URL: https://moodle.ucl.ac.uk/--><!--Course name: CCME0037: Design and Use of Artificial Intelligence in Education 25/26--><!--Course ID: 51298--><!--Author name: Yildiz Uzun--><!--Author ID: --><!--Author username: dtnvyuz--><!--Image handling: embedded--><!--Contains embedded images: 4-->
<p class="Warning">Warning: this file must be opened using Word 2020 or Word 365 in order to handle embedded images correctly.</p>
<p class="MsoTitle">Module Handbook</p><div class="chapter" id="intro"/><div class="chapter" id="207840"><h1 class="'MsoHeading1'">Module Overview</h1>
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;

<h2 class="MsoHeading2">Module Summary</h2>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">Through a mixture of theoretical and practical insights, the <strong>Design and Use of AI in Education (DUTE_AI)</strong> module offers multiple different perspectives on AI and technology design and use for education. Technology as a construct is explored in its broadest terms to allow students to appreciate its continuous presence and role in the evolution of human cognition and both practical and intellectual innovation. However, the particular focus of the module is on learning analytics tools, approaches and technologies. Pertinent theoretical perspectives of learning are summarised to provide the grounding necessary for understanding different technological designs and implementations and to furnish students with an informed basis for critiquing and evaluating technologies as educational tools in varied contexts of use.  </p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">DUTE_AI module covers literature from learning analytics, AI in Education, and the learning sciences as well as the common statistical and machine learning approaches for educational data in real-world settings. In addition, the module discusses data-informed decision-making in the design and implementation of educational technologies as well as their evaluation in terms of their efficacy in achieving their intended educational outcomes in real-world contexts.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">Students will be engaging in all three social planes of learning. </p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">1) At the cohort level, students will engage in weekly discussions on the most significant promises and challenges of educational technologies.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">2) At the group level, students will work in interdisciplinary groups to construct their own potential solutions to significant educational challenges. Design solutions will be discussed in weekly face-to-face group activities and zoom calls through brainstorming and prototyping activities. </p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">3) At the individual level, students will work on a shared Python code to run their analyses and models, and document their individual reflections about the weekly content covered in the module. </p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText" dir="ltr">Students will use and experience some of the cutting-edge educational technologies as part of their engagement with the module as a way of promoting reflection on the current technology-enhanced learning practices.</p>&#13;

<h2 class="MsoHeading2">Module Syllabus</h2>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText">This is an overview of the syllabus on DUTE_AI.  Details of each session and the related activities and readings will be published on an ongoing basis.</p>&#13;
<div class="editor-indent" style="margin-left: 30px;"> </div>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 1: </strong><strong>Introduction to Module's AI and Analytics Interventions</strong><strong style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">: </strong><strong style="font-size: 0.9375rem;"> </strong><span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">This introductory session will h</span><span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">ighlight and discuss with students how data are utilised to support the delivery of the module with learning analytics and AI.</span></p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 2:</strong> <strong>Data as the Key Affordance of Technology for Learning:</strong> This session will focus on introducing a broad definition of technology and introducing data as a key affordance of technology for supporting human learning and education.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 3: </strong><span lang="EN-US"><strong>Richard Noss Lecture: </strong></span>This week will include a special Lecture on <em>Learning Through Productive Failure In The GenAI Age</em> by Professor Manu Kapur, along with a bonus lecture introducing the fundamentals of Large Language Models.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 4: </strong><span lang="EN-US"><strong>Learning Analytics: </strong></span>This week will focus on introducing Learning Analytics, examining the relationship between learning theories, learning design, digital data traces, and learning analytics.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 5: Multimodal Learning Analytics:</strong>  This week you will have an experience of using a multimodal approach to the design and use of technology for education.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 6: </strong><strong>Value-driven Nature of Data and Performance Measures: </strong>This session will discuss the value-driven nature of artefacts including data and algorithms and will cover some methodologies to measure the success and failure of learning analytics models in practice. </p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 7:  From Prediction to Generation | Natural Language Processing (NLP) in Education: </strong>This session extends predictive thinking to analyse and generate text-based data through Natural Language Processing.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 8: AI and a Vision for Hybrid Intelligence Systems: </strong>This lecture will challenge the prevalent narrow conceptualization of AI as stochastic tools, highlighting two common fallacies in current discourse about AI&#8217;s transformative potential in education. The lecture delves into three conceptualizations of AI in education: the externalization of cognition, the internalization of AI models to influence human thought processes, and the extension of human cognition via tightly integrated human-AI systems.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 9: </strong><strong>Evidence-informed Implementations of AI in Education: </strong><span style="font-size: 1rem; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, 'Noto Sans', 'Liberation Sans', sans-serif, 'Apple Color Emoji', 'Segoe UI Emoji', 'Segoe UI Symbol', 'Noto Color Emoji';">This session will cover the importance of generating evidence about the effectiveness of AI implementations in education, challenges associated with evaluating the performance of generative AI tool implementations, and learn about how to use logic models to design an evaluation of AI in Education interventions.</span></p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><strong>Session 10: Student presentations and assessment: </strong>In this session, the students will present their group designs to illustrate different methods, concepts and ideas explored during DUTE_AI in practice. We will also focus on the preparation for the assignment and clarify any questions.</p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<div>&#13;
<div> </div>&#13;
</div>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><br/><br/></p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"><br/><br/></p>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/></div><div class="chapter" id="207836"><h1 class="'MsoHeading1'">Specification</h1>
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;

<h2 class="MsoHeading2">Design and Use of Artificial Intelligence in Education</h2>&#13;
<p class="MsoBodyText"/>&#13;

<table style="width: 69.7823%; height: 529.75px; border-width: 1px; border-style: solid;"><thead>&#13;
<tr style="height: 44.5px;">&#13;

